"Designing an experiment on queue information|Psychology|Two psychology students and their tutor discuss whether a sign changes how long a queue feels. A countdown might reassure people, but a wrong estimate could create frustration. Their first idea was to compare two supermarkets; the tutor points out that different customers and staffing would make the comparison unfair. They instead plan alternate conditions within a single setting.|The tutor asks them to measure perceived waiting as well as actual waiting, because the two are not equivalent. A short question after service avoids interrupting the queue itself. The students must record unexpected interruptions and exclude only those periods described in their plan. Removing inconvenient results afterwards would make the findings look stronger without making them more trustworthy.|The study location=campus cafe;The intervention=countdown sign;The control display=blank screen;The allocation method=alternating periods;The outcome measure=perceived waiting;The timing device=stopwatch;The feedback moment=after service;The excluded event=fire drill;The minimum sample=120;The written safeguard=analysis plan",
